lty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-917db9fa-14aa-58e3-8aee-dd6069fe2889" "answer-field-b4fce734-0f4e-5bea-8f2f-5a31ade44110"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-917db9fa-14aa-58e3-8aee-dd6069fe2889" :answer-field/id #uuid "917db9fa-14aa-58e3-8aee-dd6069fe2889" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-9e167622-6728-5bab-98df-dcc27cb319d0" :answer-field/choices ["answer-9e167622-6728-5bab-98df-dcc27cb319d0"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9e167622-6728-5bab-98df-dcc27cb319d0" :answer/id #uuid "9e167622-6728-5bab-98df-dcc27cb319d0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\cos\\theta-3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-b4fce734-0f4e-5bea-8f2f-5a31ade44110" :answer-field/id #uuid "b4fce734-0f4e-5bea-8f2f-5a31ade44110" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-b1a663f3-d97a-5352-8f66-80fa9894fe7b" :answer-field/choices ["answer-b1a663f3-d97a-5352-8f66-80fa9894fe7b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b1a663f3-d97a-5352-8f66-80fa9894fe7b" :answer/id #uuid "b1a663f3-d97a-5352-8f66-80fa9894fe7b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6\\sin\\theta+4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-11028c29-bebb-5855-afbb-0a0cead7b8d3" :question/id #uuid "11028c29-bebb-5855-afbb-0a0cead7b8d3" :question/math-academy-id "q-79288" :question/problem "![](images/13/13036c467cbe4fdd245b12c481de08ac1eb14bbfcc50653309a913ab1e9d8463.png)\n\nWhich of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$ generate the ellipse shown above?" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the point $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +h,\\,y=b\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +k,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, the ellipse is centered at the point $(4,-2),$ $a=7$ and $b=4.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the ellipse are\n\n$$\nx=7\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +4,\\,y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -2,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-1f1d937e-944d-5b73-91e1-cf60f6eae5c2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-1f1d937e-944d-5b73-91e1-cf60f6eae5c2" :answer-field/id #uuid "1f1d937e-944d-5b73-91e1-cf60f6eae5c2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-489612fd-3d5d-549b-bf4c-cf9add26f34a" :answer-field/choices ["answer-b150be25-e003-5b10-9396-dabb3aa842c3" "answer-0f782b49-6ee2-54b8-a0c6-e6dbea2f2953" "answer-0c2889b6-5645-5d8e-be53-45d82541f5ca" "answer-489612fd-3d5d-549b-bf4c-cf9add26f34a" "answer-23c366f0-0d8c-5687-b051-7995725ed6fd"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b150be25-e003-5b10-9396-dabb3aa842c3" :answer/id #uuid "b150be25-e003-5b10-9396-dabb3aa842c3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x=49\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -4,\\,y=16\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0f782b49-6ee2-54b8-a0c6-e6dbea2f2953" :answer/id #uuid "0f782b49-6ee2-54b8-a0c6-e6dbea2f2953" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2,\\,y=7\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0c2889b6-5645-5d8e-be53-45d82541f5ca" :answer/id #uuid "0c2889b6-5645-5d8e-be53-45d82541f5ca" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +4,\\,y=7\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-489612fd-3d5d-549b-bf4c-cf9add26f34a" :answer/id #uuid "489612fd-3d5d-549b-bf4c-cf9add26f34a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x=7\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +4,\\,y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-23c366f0-0d8c-5687-b051-7995725ed6fd" :answer/id #uuid "23c366f0-0d8c-5687-b051-7995725ed6fd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x=7\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -2,\\,y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-df871080-1b98-5262-9947-842390c2ab3d" :question/id #uuid "df871080-1b98-5262-9947-842390c2ab3d" :question/math-academy-id "q-79274" :question/problem "![](images/00/008a79d8328b5b58cdc68a39a921125a1efa7e0b46a663b1ede9dc6773fb48e8.png)\n\nThe quarter-ellipse above is defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $\\theta ?$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=b\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=3$ and $b=1.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the *full* ellipse are\n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a quarter-ellipse, we associate the parameter $\\theta $ with the central angle formed by a point $(x,y)$ on the elipse and the $x$-axis. \n\n![](images/82/82436a7f248ed63683a3164d8c372433abb6f919567defa05700ebf88c50308f.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $\\theta $ to $[0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}]$, we get the quarter-ellipse.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the quarter-ellipse are \n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}].\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-7b514b94-9f5f-5d34-a8ae-19775f141b95"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-7b514b94-9f5f-5d34-a8ae-19775f141b95" :answer-field/id #uuid "7b514b94-9f5f-5d34-a8ae-19775f141b95" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-daa6d892-2a21-500d-8443-31bed71ef335" :answer-field/choices ["answer-daa6d892-2a21-500d-8443-31bed71ef335" "answer-886ec113-7b60-5e77-8efe-36c5c735707f" "answer-d14209db-88dd-5983-9126-30629f8a80b5" "answer-ad707447-089b-5bd7-be1f-edc935e252bb" "answer-1b0268f3-fae3-58df-a8bd-8076695ac030"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-daa6d892-2a21-500d-8443-31bed71ef335" :answer/id #uuid "daa6d892-2a21-500d-8443-31bed71ef335" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-886ec113-7b60-5e77-8efe-36c5c735707f" :answer/id #uuid "886ec113-7b60-5e77-8efe-36c5c735707f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\theta \\in [0,\\pi ]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d14209db-88dd-5983-9126-30629f8a80b5" :answer/id #uuid "d14209db-88dd-5983-9126-30629f8a80b5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\theta \\in [\\frac{3\\pi }{2},2\\pi ]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ad707447-089b-5bd7-be1f-edc935e252bb" :answer/id #uuid "ad707447-089b-5bd7-be1f-edc935e252bb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\theta \\in [\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi ]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1b0268f3-fae3-58df-a8bd-8076695ac030" :answer/id #uuid "1b0268f3-fae3-58df-a8bd-8076695ac030" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\theta \\in [\\pi ,2\\pi ]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-f6c896d6-3993-5114-bd3a-88e176c55230" :question/id #uuid "f6c896d6-3993-5114-bd3a-88e176c55230" :question/math-academy-id "q-106401" :question/problem "![](images/41/410d6ec438e066487cd6de9d4bcbf429362a5a86d5893e7820a832d2fffb9bbd.png)\n\nThe quarter-ellipse above is defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $\\theta ?$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=b\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=2$ and $b=3.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the *full* ellipse are\n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a quarter-ellipse, we associate the parameter $\\theta $ with the central angle formed by a point $(x,y)$ on the elipse and the $x$-axis. \n\n![](images/06/06e4a1a4d50943deff0b50eb510ca7ad4676ad6015a9503f7ce279f249078858.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $\\theta $ to $[0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}]$, we get the quarter-ellipse.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the quarter-ellipse are \n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}].\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-c75e67ad-7886-5cac-bd54-994109d0b711"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-c75e67ad-7886-5cac-bd54-994109d0b711" :answer-field/id #uuid "c75e67ad-7886-5cac-bd54-994109d0b711" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-07f7a51d-051b-5f61-a319-e4c035a51a2b" :answer-field/choices ["answer-07f7a51d-051b-5f61-a319-e4c035a51a2b" "answer-5aeea71b-7af8-5a52-acd0-a5f37cb0cba0" "answer-279c72e9-1a9f-5c00-887d-cffe33962772" "answer-c6715b4c-89dd-5083-9a48-108fad9694a0" "answer-baf87542-5fd0-51dc-a1ee-ff620017e69a"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-07f7a51d-051b-5f61-a319-e4c035a51a2b" :answer/id #uuid "07f7a51d-051b-5f61-a319-e4c035a51a2b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5aeea71b-7af8-5a52-acd0-a5f37cb0cba0" :answer/id #uuid "5aeea71b-7af8-5a52-acd0-a5f37cb0cba0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\theta \\in [0,\\pi ]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-279c72e9-1a9f-5c00-887d-cffe33962772" :answer/id #uuid "279c72e9-1a9f-5c00-887d-cffe33962772" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\theta \\in [\\frac{3\\pi }{2},2\\pi ]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c6715b4c-89dd-5083-9a48-108fad9694a0" :answer/id #uuid "c6715b4c-89dd-5083-9a48-108fad9694a0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\theta \\in [\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi ]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-baf87542-5fd0-51dc-a1ee-ff620017e69a" :answer/id #uuid "baf87542-5fd0-51dc-a1ee-ff620017e69a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\theta \\in [\\pi ,2\\pi ]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-5bd7c7cd-dbab-5060-9d8d-5c720fc231fd" :question/id #uuid "5bd7c7cd-dbab-5060-9d8d-5c720fc231fd" :question/math-academy-id "q-79277" :question/problem "![](images/cc/cc9e723494b5d592b97ff1b4795eb1bc0ca7be0c4608591792eb2ac5515a9811.png)\n\nThe quarter-ellipse above is defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $\\theta ?$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=b\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=1$ and $b=3.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the *full* ellipse are\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a quarter-ellipse, we associate the parameter $\\theta $ with the central angle formed by a point $(x,y)$ on the elipse and the $x$-axis. \n\n![](images/6e/6eeff954ea6f159a3bdc781361da094319bcf06a5365d12e894228bb172a16e6.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $\\theta $ to $[\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi ]$, we get the quarter-ellipse.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the quarter-ellipse are \n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi ].\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-8e283730-f85b-5a90-97ac-ce8837023433"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-8e283730-f85b-5a90-97ac-ce8837023433" :answer-field/id #uuid "8e283730-f85b-5a90-97ac-ce8837023433" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-7a45c712-d256-5991-8b85-1e21c37c1b60" :answer-field/choices ["answer-a834dcff-125a-5c3d-ad79-8fa365d3091d" "answer-98499a59-a54e-55ee-813f-b3c78cd0eb6c" "answer-9d04eaff-79c1-5cde-9560-3f197faa247f" "answer-7a45c712-d256-5991-8b85-1e21c37c1b60" "answer-748ac3a5-85cc-5953-936a-bedc1c90f253"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a834dcff-125a-5c3d-ad79-8fa365d3091d" :answer/id #uuid "a834dcff-125a-5c3d-ad79-8fa365d3091d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-98499a59-a54e-55ee-813f-b3c78cd0eb6c" :answer/id #uuid "98499a59-a54e-55ee-813f-b3c78cd0eb6c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9d04eaff-79c1-5cde-9560-3f197faa247f" :answer/id #uuid "9d04eaff-79c1-5cde-9560-3f197faa247f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\theta \\in [\\frac{\\pi }{2},2\\pi ]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7a45c712-d256-5991-8b85-1e21c37c1b60" :answer/id #uuid "7a45c712-d256-5991-8b85-1e21c37c1b60" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\theta \\in [\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi ]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-748ac3a5-85cc-5953-936a-bedc1c90f253" :answer/id #uuid "748ac3a5-85cc-5953-936a-bedc1c90f253" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\theta \\in [\\pi ,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-e7435811-f6b1-5cc6-8105-467790c4f32b" :question/id #uuid "e7435811-f6b1-5cc6-8105-467790c4f32b" :question/math-academy-id "q-15801" :question/problem "Determine the Cartesian equation of the ellipse\n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=7\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation of the ellipse, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{x}{4} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{4}^{2}} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{16} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =7\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{y}{7} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{{7}^{2}} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{49} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nAdding the two equations and using ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{16}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{49} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{16}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{49}=1.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-4318b039-4a2e-5f72-894b-9e7f5517fbea"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-4318b039-4a2e-5f72-894b-9e7f5517fbea" :answer-field/id #uuid "4318b039-4a2e-5f72-894b-9e7f5517fbea" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-2ca34a0e-8d0c-5de6-a6da-86fe375f2f1b" :answer-field/choices ["answer-2ca34a0e-8d0c-5de6-a6da-86fe375f2f1b" "answer-383804ba-6e24-59d1-8b86-33a991e9821e" "answer-0e4db871-9264-5abb-8b35-f8cefa4d7edc" "answer-389fd57e-2d25-5ed5-a585-2bc467281b50" "answer-5dd2ee4e-8bd6-545a-9a0a-ae52bffe93ef"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2ca34a0e-8d0c-5de6-a6da-86fe375f2f1b" :answer/id #uuid "2ca34a0e-8d0c-5de6-a6da-86fe375f2f1b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{16}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{49}=1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-383804ba-6e24-59d1-8b86-33a991e9821e" :answer/id #uuid "383804ba-6e24-59d1-8b86-33a991e9821e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{49}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16}=1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0e4db871-9264-5abb-8b35-f8cefa4d7edc" :answer/id #uuid "0e4db871-9264-5abb-8b35-f8cefa4d7edc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{49}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16}=1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-389fd57e-2d25-5ed5-a585-2bc467281b50" :answer/id #uuid "389fd57e-2d25-5ed5-a585-2bc467281b50" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{7}=1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5dd2ee4e-8bd6-545a-9a0a-ae52bffe93ef" :answer/id #uuid "5dd2ee4e-8bd6-545a-9a0a-ae52bffe93ef" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{49}=1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-b0a23b4a-b8a9-57f6-be01-ea66f5dec94e" :question/id #uuid "b0a23b4a-b8a9-57f6-be01-ea66f5dec94e" :question/math-academy-id "q-327807" :question/problem "The Cartesian equation of the ellipse defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=5\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=3\\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .\n$$\n\nis given by\n\n{{field-1}}\n$=1.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation of the ellipse, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =5\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{x}{5\\sqrt{2}} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{(5\\sqrt{2})^{2}} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{50} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =3\\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{y}{3\\sqrt{7}} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{(3\\sqrt{7})^{2}} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{63} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations and using ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{50}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{63}={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1.\n$$\n\nSo, the Cartesian equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{50}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{63}=1.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-0c37ef4d-2cd6-55e8-930f-488347a97b91"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-0c37ef4d-2cd6-55e8-930f-488347a97b91" :answer-field/id #uuid "0c37ef4d-2cd6-55e8-930f-488347a97b91" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-e7e03f86-abab-5944-93ad-4c56076946d7" :answer-field/choices ["answer-e7e03f86-abab-5944-93ad-4c56076946d7"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e7e03f86-abab-5944-93ad-4c56076946d7" :answer/id #uuid "e7e03f86-abab-5944-93ad-4c56076946d7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{x^2}{50}+\\frac{y^2}{63}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-0d95a82d-3596-5013-a69c-59119b644955" :question/id #uuid "0d95a82d-3596-5013-a69c-59119b644955" :question/math-academy-id "q-327800" :question/problem "The Cartesian equation of the ellipse defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -4,\\,y=3\\sqrt{3}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .\n$$\n\nis given by\n\n{{field-1}}\n$=1.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation of the ellipse, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -4 \\\\ \\frac{x+4}{5} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(x+4)^{2}}{{5}^{2}} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(x+4)^{2}}{25} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =3\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2 \\\\ \\frac{y-2}{3\\sqrt{3}} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{(3\\sqrt{3})^{2}} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{27} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations and using ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x+4)^{2}}{25}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{27} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the Cartesian equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x+4)^{2}}{25}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{27}=1.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-b240cd70-b283-509c-882a-a63367769e6b"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-b240cd70-b283-509c-882a-a63367769e6b" :answer-field/id #uuid "b240cd70-b283-509c-882a-a63367769e6b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-7fa5b866-e128-5753-a445-f1b463ce86be" :answer-field/choices ["answer-7fa5b866-e128-5753-a445-f1b463ce86be"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7fa5b866-e128-5753-a445-f1b463ce86be" :answer/id #uuid "7fa5b866-e128-5753-a445-f1b463ce86be" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{(x+4)^2}{25}+\\frac{(y-2)^2}{27}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186302354 :question/problem "The Cartesian equation of the ellipse defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=6\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=11\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .\n$$\n\nis given by\n\n{{field-1}}\n$=1.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186301369 :answer-field/correct "answer-611e2833-ce93-5c10-9e5b-40b95d3ca49a" :answer-field/choices ["answer-611e2833-ce93-5c10-9e5b-40b95d3ca49a"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-611e2833-ce93-5c10-9e5b-40b95d3ca49a" :answer/id #uuid "611e2833-ce93-5c10-9e5b-40b95d3ca49a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{x^2}{72}+\\frac{y^2}{121}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186152363 :activity/expected-seconds 480.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id 17592186161990 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-8014f4b5-d696-5a4e-9a7f-b8b8bdffa3fa"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-8014f4b5-d696-5a4e-9a7f-b8b8bdffa3fa" :question/id #uuid "8014f4b5-d696-5a4e-9a7f-b8b8bdffa3fa" :question/math-academy-id "q-94255" :question/problem "$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{7} & \\,∗\\, & -\\frac{2}{7} \\\\ \\frac{6}{7} & \\,∗\\, & \\frac{3}{7} \\\\ \\frac{2}{7} & \\,∗\\, & -\\frac{6}{7}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $P$ shown above. Given that $A=PD{P}^{T}$ is an orthogonal diagonalization of a $3\\times 3$ symmetric matrix $A$ for some diagonal matrix $D,$ which of the following could be the second column of the matrix $P?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PD{P}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix.\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nSince  $A=PD{P}^{T}$ is an orthogonal diagonalization of a $3\\times 3$ symmetric matrix $A,$ the matrix $P$ must be a $3\\times 3$ orthogonal matrix. This means that \n\n- the second column must be orthogonal to the first and third columns, and\n\n- the norm of the second column must be equal to $1.$\n\nAmong the given options, only the vector $[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{6}{7} \\\\ \\frac{2}{7} \\\\ \\frac{3}{7}\\end{aligned}]$ has both properties." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-0955e1bc-1175-5f17-8397-f362af82c199"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-0955e1bc-1175-5f17-8397-f362af82c199" :answer-field/id #uuid "0955e1bc-1175-5f17-8397-f362af82c199" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-23c04395-ce43-5d82-ac75-6010c877b66c" :answer-field/choices ["answer-c3865f0a-5c7f-5b9d-a949-8f12089e2dba" "answer-cf66ad90-d7c5-51af-a6d3-7f6366428596" "answer-23c04395-ce43-5d82-ac75-6010c877b66c" "answer-18fc3a40-e1d0-55d4-9ce7-fda3abb23ec2" "answer-983bec27-e9e7-559c-97c6-f07993159084"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c3865f0a-5c7f-5b9d-a949-8f12089e2dba" :answer/id #uuid "c3865f0a-5c7f-5b9d-a949-8f12089e2dba" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cf66ad90-d7c5-51af-a6d3-7f6366428596" :answer/id #uuid "cf66ad90-d7c5-51af-a6d3-7f6366428596" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-23c04395-ce43-5d82-ac75-6010c877b66c" :answer/id #uuid "23c04395-ce43-5d82-ac75-6010c877b66c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{6}{7} \\\\ \\frac{2}{7} \\\\ \\frac{3}{7}\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-18fc3a40-e1d0-55d4-9ce7-fda3abb23ec2" :answer/id #uuid "18fc3a40-e1d0-55d4-9ce7-fda3abb23ec2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{7} \\\\ -\\frac{6}{7} \\\\ \\frac{3}{7}\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-983bec27-e9e7-559c-97c6-f07993159084" :answer/id #uuid "983bec27-e9e7-559c-97c6-f07993159084" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{5} \\\\ \\frac{2}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{6}{5}\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186161989 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-d509446a-8a97-556c-a812-ab4e4c09b27f" "question-4abf3f5b-ec82-5ed8-874d-b7cd0f7cd357"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-d509446a-8a97-556c-a812-ab4e4c09b27f" :question/id #uuid "d509446a-8a97-556c-a812-ab4e4c09b27f" :question/math-academy-id "q-94343" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -2\\end{aligned}],\\,D=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. \nGiven that $D={P}^{T}\\,AP,$ where $P$ is an orthogonal matrix, which of the following could be the first and second columns of the matrix $P?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PD{P}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix. Equivalently,\n\n$$\nD={P}^{T}AP.\n$$\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nFirst, note that $A$ is symmetric and $D$ is diagonal. Therefore, $D={P}^{T}\\,AP$ gives an orthogonal diagonalization of $A.$\n\nFrom the diagonal matrix $D$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=2,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=2,$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=-3.$ So, the first and second columns of $P$ correspond to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}.$ \n\nNow, we need to find  eigenvectors that correspond to ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}=2.$ Computing $A-2I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-2I & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -2\\end{aligned}]-2[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-2I)x=0$ gives two eigenvectors: \n\n$\\,$ ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\n\nNotice that eigenvectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are  orthogonal. So, we divide ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ by their respective norms, to obtain the first and second columns of $P:$  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ \\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖} & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ \\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-1863d021-7fed-5c34-89fc-7b5b9dc0e6f3"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-1863d021-7fed-5c34-89fc-7b5b9dc0e6f3" :answer-field/id #uuid "1863d021-7fed-5c34-89fc-7b5b9dc0e6f3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-cca46d49-7d23-5e85-ab60-d67226f0d85c" :answer-field/choices ["answer-dc509a11-c0ea-5576-b20f-2da1fad02d0f" "answer-8b13dc4d-fe81-56ad-a124-849d6ef3870b" "answer-462faacd-a6dd-5241-a9c7-f412605afeaa" "answer-cca46d49-7d23-5e85-ab60-d67226f0d85c" "answer-8e579e40-44ae-5898-93ae-2dd1031a7f96"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-dc509a11-c0ea-5576-b20f-2da1fad02d0f" :answer/id #uuid "dc509a11-c0ea-5576-b20f-2da1fad02d0f" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8b13dc4d-fe81-56ad-a124-849d6ef3870b" :answer/id #uuid "8b13dc4d-fe81-56ad-a124-849d6ef3870b" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-462faacd-a6dd-5241-a9c7-f412605afeaa" :answer/id #uuid "462faacd-a6dd-5241-a9c7-f412605afeaa" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\ 1 \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cca46d49-7d23-5e85-ab60-d67226f0d85c" :answer/id #uuid "cca46d49-7d23-5e85-ab60-d67226f0d85c" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ \\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8e579e40-44ae-5898-93ae-2dd1031a7f96" :answer/id #uuid "8e579e40-44ae-5898-93ae-2dd1031a7f96" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{6} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{6} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{3}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-4abf3f5b-ec82-5ed8-874d-b7cd0f7cd357" :question/id #uuid "4abf3f5b-ec82-5ed8-874d-b7cd0f7cd357" :question/math-academy-id "q-94348" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,D=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. \nGiven that $D={P}^{T}\\,AP,$ where $P$ is an orthogonal matrix, which of the following could be the first and second columns of the matrix $P?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PD{P}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix. Equivalently,\n\n$$\nD={P}^{T}AP.\n$$\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nFirst, note that $A$ is symmetric and $D$ is diagonal. Therefore, $D={P}^{T}\\,AP$ gives an orthogonal diagonalization of $A.$\n\nFrom the diagonal matrix $D$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=1,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=1,$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=3.$ So, the first and second columns of $P$ correspond to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}.$ \n\nNow, we need to find eigenvectors that correspond to ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}=1.$ Computing $A-1I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-I & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-I)x=0$ gives two eigenvectors: \n\n$\\,$ ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$\n\nNotice that eigenvectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are  orthogonal. So, we divide ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ by their respective norms to obtain the first and second columns of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖} & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ \\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖} & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-bcb2a00c-a32a-58e5-9c52-417ed8779317"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-bcb2a00c-a32a-58e5-9c52-417ed8779317" :answer-field/id #uuid "bcb2a00c-a32a-58e5-9c52-417ed8779317" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-e7be7e91-5a14-584b-85a0-f55343f44596" :answer-field/choices ["answer-56942e9a-163a-52c1-a3a7-b6de028f8362" "answer-e7be7e91-5a14-584b-85a0-f55343f44596" "answer-044983dd-f235-51e0-a331-008fc0d161b4" "answer-3e2993d9-6ce2-5240-a5d7-087f9b14447b" "answer-d8ddd1b3-cfb4-555f-9ce5-59938f691e9e"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-56942e9a-163a-52c1-a3a7-b6de028f8362" :answer/id #uuid "56942e9a-163a-52c1-a3a7-b6de028f8362" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e7be7e91-5a14-584b-85a0-f55343f44596" :answer/id #uuid "e7be7e91-5a14-584b-85a0-f55343f44596" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-044983dd-f235-51e0-a331-008fc0d161b4" :answer/id #uuid "044983dd-f235-51e0-a331-008fc0d161b4" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3e2993d9-6ce2-5240-a5d7-087f9b14447b" :answer/id #uuid "3e2993d9-6ce2-5240-a5d7-087f9b14447b" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{6} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{6}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d8ddd1b3-cfb4-555f-9ce5-59938f691e9e" :answer/id #uuid "d8ddd1b3-cfb4-555f-9ce5-59938f691e9e" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ \\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186163249 :tutorial/content "Let's recall the algorithm for diagonalizing a $3\\times 3$ symmetric matrix $A.$\n\n- **Step 1**: Find the eigenvalues of $A.$\n\n- **Step 2**: Find a basis of  $\\mathbb{R}^3$ consisting of unit orthogonal eigenvectors of $A.$\n\n- **Step 3**: Construct the matrices $D$ and $P.$\n\nNote the following important points regarding step 2 of this process:\n\n- If $A$ has mutually distinct eigenvalues, the eigenvectors will automatically be orthogonal, so we only need to normalize them.\n\n- If $A$ has *repeated* eigenvalues, then the eigenvectors corresponding to these repeated eigenvalues *may not* be orthogonal. Therefore, we have to apply the Gram-Schmidt process to obtain orthogonal vectors.\n\nFor example, consider the following matrix:\n\n![](images/28/2807ddab01a673d01c385b0dcc1b77d007896f803376006e7f81f639930fecdc.svg)\n\nThis matrix is symmetric. Let's apply our diagonalization algorithm.\n\n- **Step 1.**  We find the eigenvalues of $A$ by solving $\\det(A-\\lambda I)=0\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\lambda_1=4, \\qquad \\lambda_2=4, \\qquad \\lambda_3=-2\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Now, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $\\mathbb R^3.$ Seeking a nonzero solution of $|A-\\lambda_i I| = 0$ for $i=1,2,3,$ we get the corresponding eigenvectors\n![](images/95/95d0808a376ad679118ee26b9387cc3037862b74f12945db886a2ffd7738d525.svg)\nNotice that $\\mathbf{v}_1$ and $\\mathbf{v}_2$ are not orthogonal. So, we proceed using the Gram-Schmidt process. We set \n![](images/b4/b4aaec2c201e4ae0aff1054c57fdf4cc860dac1fa6bd38a9b58865b5306b8ce2.svg)\nand we find $\\mathbf{e}_2$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}_{2} & ={v}_{2}-\\frac{{v}_{2}\\cdot {e}_{1}}{{e}_{1}\\cdot {e}_{1}}{e}_{1} \\\\  & ={v}_{2}-\\frac{1}{2}{e}_{1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]-(-\\frac{1}{2})[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the vectors $\\mathbf e_1, \\mathbf e_2$ and $\\mathbf v_3$ form an *orthogonal* basis of $\\mathbb R^3.$ Dividing each eigenvector by its norm, we obtain the unit orthogonal eigenvectors:\n![](images/c9/c9a112d21b6868b583f521a73a5aa491a40ce46c365adf6ddc3f7eeb60c9f5fe.svg)\n\n- **Step 3.** We construct the diagonal matrix $D$ and the invertible matrix $P$ as follows:\n![](images/f1/f111684678ff06708513e20f22db25b4d6246dda2a8ce5f50b5905c9a13b0cbd.svg)" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186101022 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-f3134f95-ccde-508a-8ade-bbcbdfc93226"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-f3134f95-ccde-508a-8ade-bbcbdfc93226" :question/id #uuid "f3134f95-ccde-508a-8ade-bbcbdfc93226" :question/math-academy-id "q-344647" :question/problem "Find the average value ${g}_{\\text{avg}}$ of the function $g(x)=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ on the interval $[-\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{\\pi }{4}].$ Do not round the answer.\n\n${g}_{\\text{avg}}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Applying the average value formula, we find that the average value of the function is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}_{\\text{avg}} & =\\frac{1}{b-a}{\\int }_{a}^{b}g(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(\\pi /4-(-\\pi /4))}{\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4}(6\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4}(6\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\frac{2}{\\pi }(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)|}_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }[(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4})-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}))-(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-\\frac{3\\pi }{4})-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-\\frac{\\pi }{4}))] \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }[(2\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}-4\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})-(2\\cdot (-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})-4\\cdot (-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}))] \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }[(-\\sqrt{2})-(\\sqrt{2})] \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }\\cdot (-2\\sqrt{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{-4\\sqrt{2}}{\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-1e0422d8-ab75-5b1f-88c6-2c6d1cf32970"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-1e0422d8-ab75-5b1f-88c6-2c6d1cf32970" :answer-field/id #uuid "1e0422d8-ab75-5b1f-88c6-2c6d1cf32970" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-08723c80-e899-5f73-9042-d123c4781581" :answer-field/choices ["answer-08723c80-e899-5f73-9042-d123c4781581"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-08723c80-e899-5f73-9042-d123c4781581" :answer/id #uuid "08723c80-e899-5f73-9042-d123c4781581" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{4\\sqrt{2}}{\\pi}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186176020 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-70edaacc-f97d-53a0-9bf0-62992d59dc46"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-70edaacc-f97d-53a0-9bf0-62992d59dc46" :question/id #uuid "70edaacc-f97d-53a0-9bf0-62992d59dc46" :question/math-academy-id "q-353478" :question/problem "The number $y(t)$ of people who have adopted electric vehicles in a town follows the logistic growth model\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=4200y-7{y}^{2},\\,y(0)=55,\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ is the time, in years, since the observations began. According to this model, what is the maximum possible number of people who will adopt electric vehicles?\n\nThe maximum possible number of people who will adopt electric vehicles is\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "A logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=ky(a-y),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>y$ is the carrying capacity. \n\nThe carrying capacity $a$ represents the maximum possible number of people who will adopt electric vehicles. A diagram showing a typical logistic growth curve is shown below.\n\n![](images/d8/d8133f1c4203903bac606bbae61c73717e2ba2dc15a97a77f48eaaeaf4736d06.png)\n\nFirst, let's rewrite the right-hand side of the given equation so that it matches the form above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =4200y-7{y}^{2} \\\\  & =7y(600-y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom here, we see that $a=600.$ Hence, the carrying capacity is $600,$ and we conclude that the maximum possible number of people who will adopt electric vehicles is $600.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-b1a61518-8249-5d82-89b0-dd7a0d81fab9"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-b1a61518-8249-5d82-89b0-dd7a0d81fab9" :answer-field/id #uuid "b1a61518-8249-5d82-89b0-dd7a0d81fab9" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-6d330e3b-0a3b-5e96-a25c-b67e16d84426" :answer-field/choices ["answer-6d330e3b-0a3b-5e96-a25c-b67e16d84426"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6d330e3b-0a3b-5e96-a25c-b67e16d84426" :answer/id #uuid "6d330e3b-0a3b-5e96-a25c-b67e16d84426" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "600" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186129318 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-5b66c4d7-d9b1-5cb6-840d-18a9a3471ea0"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-5b66c4d7-d9b1-5cb6-840d-18a9a3471ea0" :question/id #uuid "5b66c4d7-d9b1-5cb6-840d-18a9a3471ea0" :question/math-academy-id "q-138966" :question/problem "Consider the curve defined parametrically as \n\n$$\nx={e}^{t},\\,y=2{e}^{t/2},\\,t\\in [0,4].\n$$\n\nWhat integral gives the surface area generated when this curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis?" :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi y\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince our curve is given in parametric form, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from ${t}_{1}=0$ to ${t}_{2}=4.$ \n\nComputing the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t})={e}^{t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{e}^{t/2})={e}^{t/2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}2{e}^{t/2}\\sqrt{{({e}^{t})}^{2}+{({e}^{t/2})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}{e}^{t/2}\\sqrt{{e}^{2t}+{e}^{t}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}{e}^{t/2}\\sqrt{{e}^{t}({e}^{t}+1)}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}{e}^{t/2}\\cdot {e}^{t/2}\\cdot \\sqrt{{e}^{t}+1}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}{e}^{t}\\sqrt{{e}^{t}+1}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-09078c01-f245-5447-88a1-e69ec53cbb38"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-09078c01-f245-5447-88a1-e69ec53cbb38" :answer-field/id #uuid "09078c01-f245-5447-88a1-e69ec53cbb38" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-66c00b74-0490-5e7d-8d8b-1cd2cf6001b5" :answer-field/choices ["answer-2aecc83e-62f9-5570-b3ec-23bbc0d4c666" "answer-434933b7-c938-5b99-9c27-958c4224b195" "answer-cc74ab54-9cb6-55f6-a841-1af50094d0ff" "answer-602e3020-e08a-5741-9ce8-35913e0c5082" "answer-66c00b74-0490-5e7d-8d8b-1cd2cf6001b5"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2aecc83e-62f9-5570-b3ec-23bbc0d4c666" :answer/id #uuid "2aecc83e-62f9-5570-b3ec-23bbc0d4c666" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}{e}^{t/2}\\sqrt{2{e}^{t}+4}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-434933b7-c938-5b99-9c27-958c4224b195" :answer/id #uuid "434933b7-c938-5b99-9c27-958c4224b195" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cc74ab54-9cb6-55f6-a841-1af50094d0ff" :answer/id #uuid "cc74ab54-9cb6-55f6-a841-1af50094d0ff" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}{e}^{2t}\\sqrt{{e}^{t/2}+1}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-602e3020-e08a-5741-9ce8-35913e0c5082" :answer/id #uuid "602e3020-e08a-5741-9ce8-35913e0c5082" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}{e}^{t/2}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-66c00b74-0490-5e7d-8d8b-1cd2cf6001b5" :answer/id #uuid "66c00b74-0490-5e7d-8d8b-1cd2cf6001b5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}{e}^{t}\\sqrt{{e}^{t}+1}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186360827 :question/worked-solution "For a binomial random variable $X∼B(n,p),$ where $n$ is the number of trials, and $p$ is the probability of success on each trial,  we have the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $X∼B(6,0.2),$ so $X$ has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{x})(0.2)^{x}(0.8)^{6-x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>4) & =P(X\\in \\{5,6\\}) \\\\  & =f(5)+f(6) \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{5})(0.2)^{5}(0.8)^{6-5}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{6})(0.2)^{6}(0.8)^{6-6} \\\\  & =6(0.2)^{5}(0.8)+1(0.2)^{6}(0.8)^{0} \\\\  & =0.0016.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186175978 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-dd63787d-4bed-5aec-9b0b-525bc4b676f9"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-dd63787d-4bed-5aec-9b0b-525bc4b676f9" :question/id #uuid "dd63787d-4bed-5aec-9b0b-525bc4b676f9" :question/math-academy-id "q-109259" :question/problem "![](images/d9/d9ad43f4bddaeab8327e5405193471c005962013355ec84c4a6f858640216dd7.png)\n\nConsider the arc of the curve $x=-{y}^{2}-5y-4$ that lies in the fourth quadrant between its $y$-intercepts. What integral gives the surface area generated when this arc is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis?" :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi x\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince we're given a curve in the form $x=f(y),$ we have \n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{y}_{1}}^{{y}_{2}}x\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to determine the limits of integration. To do this, we find where the curve intersects the $y$-axis by solving the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{y}^{2}-5y-4 & =0 \\\\ {y}^{2}+5y+4 & =0 \\\\ (y+1)(y+4) & =0 \\\\ y & =-4,-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we need to integrate from $y=-4$ to $y=-1.$ \n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(-{y}^{2}-5y-4)=-2y-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{y}_{1}}^{{y}_{2}}x\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{-4}^{-1}(-{y}^{2}-5y-4)\\sqrt{1+{(-2y-5)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =-2\\pi {\\int }_{-4}^{-1}({y}^{2}+5y+4)\\sqrt{26+20y+4{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =-2\\pi \\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{-4}^{-1}({y}^{2}+5y+4)\\sqrt{2{y}^{2}+10y+13}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-bfcd94c6-4a82-5d36-a7d1-d10e9ebad35a"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-bfcd94c6-4a82-5d36-a7d1-d10e9ebad35a" :answer-field/id #uuid "bfcd94c6-4a82-5d36-a7d1-d10e9ebad35a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-42dcd5e7-1244-58d1-8b4c-96176ca2b293" :answer-field/choices ["answer-3b737ea3-db83-533e-bf9c-6d4d23c19b41" "answer-42dcd5e7-1244-58d1-8b4c-96176ca2b293" "answer-7b6b74f6-b844-5d38-9b76-3abb89103049" "answer-089a813c-6c0e-5e0b-8e37-0be57131cd8f" "answer-1a442bb6-9973-5336-9114-49d50247246c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3b737ea3-db83-533e-bf9c-6d4d23c19b41" :answer/id #uuid "3b737ea3-db83-533e-bf9c-6d4d23c19b41" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2\\pi \\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{-4}^{-1}(2y+5)\\sqrt{2{y}^{2}+10y+13}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-42dcd5e7-1244-58d1-8b4c-96176ca2b293" :answer/id #uuid "42dcd5e7-1244-58d1-8b4c-96176ca2b293" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2\\pi \\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{-4}^{-1}({y}^{2}+5y+4)\\sqrt{2{y}^{2}+10y+13}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7b6b74f6-b844-5d38-9b76-3abb89103049" :answer/id #uuid "7b6b74f6-b844-5d38-9b76-3abb89103049" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2\\pi \\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{-4}^{-1}({y}^{2}+5y+4)\\sqrt{{y}^{2}+5y+1}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-089a813c-6c0e-5e0b-8e37-0be57131cd8f" :answer/id #uuid "089a813c-6c0e-5e0b-8e37-0be57131cd8f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2\\pi \\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{-5}^{-2}y\\sqrt{2{y}^{2}+10y+13}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1a442bb6-9973-5336-9114-49d50247246c" :answer/id #uuid "1a442bb6-9973-5336-9114-49d50247246c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2\\pi \\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{-5}^{-2}y\\sqrt{{y}^{2}+5y+1}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "activity-3b317c40-144c-562f-b9ab-57da1b3d6a76" :activity/id #uuid "3b317c40-144c-562f-b9ab-57da1b3d6a76" :activity/title "Quiz 3" :activity/type :activity.type/assessment :activity/course 17592186046645 :activity/steps ["step-f8310157-3f8f-548a-8d56-49dfaa06e9c5" "step-9d5c5899-4e71-5209-b06d-b42aa107689b" "step-2ca8099d-1aee-5f8c-8c6d-1f6c371b8aee" "step-06b90397-7e54-5498-8b04-22ca8c09726a" "step-64c308fc-f5c3-5287-bf12-f530ecbd2c9b"] :activity/first-step "step-f8310157-3f8f-548a-8d56-49dfaa06e9c5" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-f8310157-3f8f-548a-8d56-49dfaa06e9c5" :step/id #uuid "f8310157-3f8f-548a-8d56-49dfaa06e9c5" :step/content "question-f3134f95-ccde-508a-8ade-bbcbdfc93226" :step/next "step-9d5c5899-4e71-5209-b06d-b42aa107689b" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-9d5c5899-4e71-5209-b06d-b42aa107689b" :step/id #uuid "9d5c5899-4e71-5209-b06d-b42aa107689b" :step/content "question-70edaacc-f97d-53a0-9bf0-62992d59dc46" :step/next "step-2ca8099d-1aee-5f8c-8c6d-1f6c371b8aee" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-2ca8099d-1aee-5f8c-8c6d-1f6c371b8aee" :step/id #uuid "2ca8099d-1aee-5f8c-8c6d-1f6c371b8aee" :step/content "question-5b66c4d7-d9b1-5cb6-840d-18a9a3471ea0" :step/next "step-06b90397-7e54-5498-8b04-22ca8c09726a" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-06b90397-7e54-5498-8b04-22ca8c09726a" :step/id #uuid "06b90397-7e54-5498-8b04-22ca8c09726a" :step/content 17592186360827 :step/next "step-64c308fc-f5c3-5287-bf12-f530ecbd2c9b" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-64c308fc-f5c3-5287-bf12-f530ecbd2c9b" :step/id #uuid "64c308fc-f5c3-5287-bf12-f530ecbd2c9b" :step/content "question-dd63787d-4bed-5aec-9b0b-525bc4b676f9" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} [:db/retract 17592186107635 :answer-field/choices 17592186107636] [:db/retract 17592186107635 :answer-field/choices 17592186107637] [:db/retract 17592186107635 :answer-field/choices 17592186107638] [:db/retract 17592186107635 :answer-field/choices 17592186107639] [:db/retract 17592186107635 :answer-field/choices 17592186107640] {:db/id 17592186110457 :question/worked-solution "Any point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta$ as follows:\n\n$$\nx = \\cos\\theta, \\qquad y = \\sin\\theta.\n$$\n\nWe're given that $x=\\dfrac{2}{3}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\cos\\theta = \\dfrac{2}{3}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $\\sin\\theta\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\cos\\theta = \\dfrac{2}{3}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{4}{9}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{5}{9}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we select the positive answer since $\\sin\\theta$ is positive in the first quadrant. \n\nSo our final answer is $\\sin\\theta = \\dfrac{\\sqrt 5}{3}.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186110756 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-fb5c025a-0d14-591c-9102-8c187483f1d5" "question-40216ba0-427a-539f-9353-a0236ab249d0"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186110458 :question/worked-solution "Again, any point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta$ as follows:\n\n$$\nx = \\cos\\theta, \\qquad y = \\sin\\theta.\n$$\n\nWe're given that $x=\\dfrac{2\\sqrt 6}{7}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\cos\\theta = \\dfrac{2\\sqrt 6}{7}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $\\sin\\theta\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\cos\\theta = \\dfrac{2\\sqrt 6}{7}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{(\\frac{2\\sqrt{6}}{7})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{24}{49}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{25}{49}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{5}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe select the positive answer since $\\sin\\theta$ is positive in the first quadrant. So, $\\sin\\theta = \\dfrac{5}{7}.$\n\nFinally, knowing that $\\csc\\theta = \\dfrac{1}{\\sin\\theta},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\csc\\theta = \\dfrac{1}{\\left(\\dfrac{5}{7}\\right)} = \\dfrac{7}{5}.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186110758 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-d952daa8-27a5-56d3-8d17-8e3ce2fc3807" "question-67eb7841-2530-5536-9fe7-7fe1cd1941b8"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186110459 :question/worked-solution "We're given that $x=\\dfrac{4}{5}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\cos\\theta = \\dfrac{4}{5}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $\\sin\\theta\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\cos\\theta = \\dfrac{4}{5}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{(\\frac{4}{5})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{16}{25}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{9}{25}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{3}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe select the positive answer since $\\sin\\theta$ is positive in the first quadrant. Therefore $\\sin\\theta = \\dfrac{3}{5}.$\n\nUsing the fact that\n\n$$\n\\tan\\theta = \\dfrac{\\sin\\theta}{\\cos\\theta},\n$$\n\nwe finally get\n\n$$\n\\tan\\theta = \\dfrac{\\left(\\dfrac{3}{5}\\right)}{\\left(\\dfrac{4}{5}\\right)} = \\dfrac {3}{4}.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186110987 :question/problem "The point $A$ is located in the first quadrant and lies on the unit circle. The $y$-coordinate of $A$ is $\\frac{7}{8}.$ What is the $x$-coordinate of $A?$" :question/worked-solution "Since the point $A$ lies on the unit circle, it must satisfy the Pythagorean theorem: \n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1\n$$\n\nSubstituting $y=\\frac{7}{8}$ into the above and solving for $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{(\\frac{7}{8})}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2}+(\\frac{49}{64}) & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =1-\\frac{49}{64} \\\\ {x}^{2} & =\\frac{15}{64} \\\\ x & =\\sqrt{\\frac{15}{64}} \\\\ x & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $A$ lies in the first quadrant, $x$ must be positive, so we select the positive answer. Therefore, our final answer is $x=\\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{8}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186107635 :answer-field/correct "answer-81e941f7-2aba-5992-a3a8-a4c2f7048dd0" :answer-field/choices ["answer-56bfa8c2-dcf1-5e24-99a0-e12c2fed24ea" "answer-6c5e27f3-df3c-56b0-886c-c6883624ae4f" "answer-81e941f7-2aba-5992-a3a8-a4c2f7048dd0" "answer-6b59afa8-4240-5335-93b8-6d67f9dc609b" "answer-5ecd513e-ed5e-5b96-b8d4-8d7a690d54f3"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-56bfa8c2-dcf1-5e24-99a0-e12c2fed24ea" :answer/id #uuid "56bfa8c2-dcf1-5e24-99a0-e12c2fed24ea" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x=-\\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{8}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6c5e27f3-df3c-56b0-886c-c6883624ae4f" :answer/id #uuid "6c5e27f3-df3c-56b0-886c-c6883624ae4f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x=-\\frac{\\sqrt{31}}{8}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-81e941f7-2aba-5992-a3a8-a4c2f7048dd0" :answer/id #uuid "81e941f7-2aba-5992-a3a8-a4c2f7048dd0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x=\\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{8}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6b59afa8-4240-5335-93b8-6d67f9dc609b" :answer/id #uuid "6b59afa8-4240-5335-93b8-6d67f9dc609b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x=\\frac{\\sqrt{31}}{8}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5ecd513e-ed5e-5b96-b8d4-8d7a690d54f3" :answer/id #uuid "5ecd513e-ed5e-5b96-b8d4-8d7a690d54f3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x=\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{8}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186335783 :question/problem "![](images/5d/5d292a3598346bd05511c9c8979ba42845a88afe75a4740d8c4bdb9aa3968c87.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that the $x$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $\\frac{5}{9},$ use the Pythagorean identity to find $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/48/488ff32187c9a9a335e3782fb717242d899f622c43010662e28edcabe0e1bde3.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $x=\\frac{5}{9}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\frac{5}{9}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\frac{5}{9}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{(\\frac{5}{9})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{25}{81}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{56}{81}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{2\\sqrt{14}}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we select the positive answer since $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ is positive in the first quadrant. So our final answer is $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\frac{2\\sqrt{14}}{9}.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186335735 :question/problem "![](images/94/94b8650466747f6858b30adbb8962e6c91159466b570f536d4999e2b08c31b67.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that the $x$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $\\frac{1}{2},$ use the Pythagorean identity to find $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/cb/cb569d4b7b76699259d2167b8c930e636e3fb794b14ef25a4d909c224235d7c3.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $x=\\frac{1}{2}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{2}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{1}{4}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{3}{4}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we select the positive answer since $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ is positive in the first quadrant. So our final answer is $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-fb5c025a-0d14-591c-9102-8c187483f1d5" :question/id #uuid "fb5c025a-0d14-591c-9102-8c187483f1d5" :question/math-academy-id "q-85233" :question/problem "![](images/75/751d38a1a37afd277cbd54850abf61db898a231b49ad0d4bcaa5964bbab601e1.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that the $x$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $\\frac{4}{5},$ use the Pythagorean identity to find $\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta .$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/05/0534847b2363ff75e053b9d05736ff367f2ad6da527b7100de0018d003c4c9df.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $x=\\frac{4}{5}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\frac{4}{5}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\frac{4}{5}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{(\\frac{4}{5})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{16}{25}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{9}{25}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{3}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe select the positive answer since $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ is positive in the first quadrant. So, $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\frac{3}{5}.$\n\nFinally, knowing that $\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{3}{5})}=\\frac{5}{3}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-9d7d6654-0ebf-57f1-bbf5-229f64325368"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-9d7d6654-0ebf-57f1-bbf5-229f64325368" :answer-field/id #uuid "9d7d6654-0ebf-57f1-bbf5-229f64325368" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-0a6e02d6-f190-5b77-94b7-25463dc18fdd" :answer-field/choices ["answer-08e8b185-9e4d-524a-98a5-45013ad83009" "answer-0a6e02d6-f190-5b77-94b7-25463dc18fdd" "answer-6f15d79f-35df-5dfd-909e-20ba976f3a93" "answer-624013de-ac75-5b7a-a4d8-e9822947abad" "answer-5cae022b-6032-5575-aa99-3439709b9c23"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-08e8b185-9e4d-524a-98a5-45013ad83009" :answer/id #uuid "08e8b185-9e4d-524a-98a5-45013ad83009" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0a6e02d6-f190-5b77-94b7-25463dc18fdd" :answer/id #uuid "0a6e02d6-f190-5b77-94b7-25463dc18fdd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{5}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6f15d79f-35df-5dfd-909e-20ba976f3a93" :answer/id #uuid "6f15d79f-35df-5dfd-909e-20ba976f3a93" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{7}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-624013de-ac75-5b7a-a4d8-e9822947abad" :answer/id #uuid "624013de-ac75-5b7a-a4d8-e9822947abad" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5cae022b-6032-5575-aa99-3439709b9c23" :answer/id #uuid "5cae022b-6032-5575-aa99-3439709b9c23" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\sqrt{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-40216ba0-427a-539f-9353-a0236ab249d0" :question/id #uuid "40216ba0-427a-539f-9353-a0236ab249d0" :question/math-academy-id "q-85235" :question/problem "![](images/fd/fd812d1d33801a3ce8368feca0e7d6b87e02bcfab5d904d9b59172d95d0204e5.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that the $x$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{6},$ use the Pythagorean identity to find $\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta .$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/e0/e087efb9eb18158005722dd6628cc8f19b22a2b33cad8f67205b8ea398e62c54.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $x=\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{6}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{6}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{6}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{(\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{6})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{11}{36}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{25}{36}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{5}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe select the positive answer since $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ is positive in the first quadrant. So, $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\frac{5}{6}.$\n\nFinally, knowing that $\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{5}{6})}=\\frac{6}{5}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-030979c4-2ad6-5121-addd-f709ea3b08d4"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-030979c4-2ad6-5121-addd-f709ea3b08d4" :answer-field/id #uuid "030979c4-2ad6-5121-addd-f709ea3b08d4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-262dc74b-263d-5d80-9193-e12e8b9113bd" :answer-field/choices ["answer-e0610972-c22b-5f94-966b-2d4ed2ec3bd5" "answer-262dc74b-263d-5d80-9193-e12e8b9113bd" "answer-e423743e-6949-5391-8f90-a809db7277ef" "answer-222b62ab-1bfb-546b-aab5-03561e3c701f" "answer-3fc77f0f-f146-5ca7-b666-ad8985b69724"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e0610972-c22b-5f94-966b-2d4ed2ec3bd5" :answer/id #uuid "e0610972-c22b-5f94-966b-2d4ed2ec3bd5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{5}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-262dc74b-263d-5d80-9193-e12e8b9113bd" :answer/id #uuid "262dc74b-263d-5d80-9193-e12e8b9113bd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{6}{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e423743e-6949-5391-8f90-a809db7277ef" :answer/id #uuid "e423743e-6949-5391-8f90-a809db7277ef" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-222b62ab-1bfb-546b-aab5-03561e3c701f" :answer/id #uuid "222b62ab-1bfb-546b-aab5-03561e3c701f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3fc77f0f-f146-5ca7-b666-ad8985b69724" :answer/id #uuid "3fc77f0f-f146-5ca7-b666-ad8985b69724" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\sqrt{6}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-d952daa8-27a5-56d3-8d17-8e3ce2fc3807" :question/id #uuid "d952daa8-27a5-56d3-8d17-8e3ce2fc3807" :question/math-academy-id "q-124785" :question/problem "![](images/ca/ca003331d04113119019996f15296ec2ab16fa0c14227a12f5f421c4c4103746.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that the $y$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3},$ use the Pythagorean identity to find $\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta .$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/f0/f0c1c9df42fc2ed772353baec0f81e796485d8bf3c1cd9dde234e74e39a04c56.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $y=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{2}{9}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{7}{9}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe select the positive answer since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ is positive in the first quadrant. So, $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{3}$\n\nKnowing that $\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta =\\frac{(\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{3})}{(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3})}=\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{\\sqrt{2}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we rationalize the denominator.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{\\sqrt{2}}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{7\\cdot 2}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{14}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-e825c5c1-d92a-52fc-8cd7-189d7de3d71e"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-e825c5c1-d92a-52fc-8cd7-189d7de3d71e" :answer-field/id #uuid "e825c5c1-d92a-52fc-8cd7-189d7de3d71e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-78b7c07c-ec2e-5b2b-8de0-b1f928e490af" :answer-field/choices ["answer-4662ff5c-1779-587c-8818-862f89cec631" "answer-78b7c07c-ec2e-5b2b-8de0-b1f928e490af" "answer-57b912a8-d2ae-5a4c-a949-6c6b3b4e2b7b" "answer-c78c0d6a-5d3e-5010-92bc-68ad33e022bb" "answer-9d3c24fb-cfb1-5483-9975-5fe28ec66e17"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4662ff5c-1779-587c-8818-862f89cec631" :answer/id #uuid "4662ff5c-1779-587c-8818-862f89cec631" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-78b7c07c-ec2e-5b2b-8de0-b1f928e490af" :answer/id #uuid "78b7c07c-ec2e-5b2b-8de0-b1f928e490af" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{14}}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-57b912a8-d2ae-5a4c-a949-6c6b3b4e2b7b" :answer/id #uuid "57b912a8-d2ae-5a4c-a949-6c6b3b4e2b7b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{14}}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c78c0d6a-5d3e-5010-92bc-68ad33e022bb" :answer/id #uuid "c78c0d6a-5d3e-5010-92bc-68ad33e022bb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9d3c24fb-cfb1-5483-9975-5fe28ec66e17" :answer/id #uuid "9d3c24fb-cfb1-5483-9975-5fe28ec66e17" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-67eb7841-2530-5536-9fe7-7fe1cd1941b8" :question/id #uuid "67eb7841-2530-5536-9fe7-7fe1cd1941b8" :question/math-academy-id "q-37897" :question/problem "![](images/1b/1b084e0ad229e935d70d751f0e0ddaacf5832017951a7ef8e334f27834662d5c.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that the $x$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3},$ use the Pythagorean identity to find $\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta .$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/57/5778175613309c2b4b8007291ad122b879de1cd409ad3513f3f786c8f4885927.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $x=\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{(\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{5}{9}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{4}{9}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe select the positive answer since $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ is positive in the first quadrant. So, $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\frac{2}{3}.$\n\nKnowing that $\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =\\frac{(\\frac{2}{3})}{(\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3})}=\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we rationalize the denominator.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-0b703d74-20ff-5a85-bdd0-120e273b7e1a"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-0b703d74-20ff-5a85-bdd0-120e273b7e1a" :answer-field/id #uuid "0b703d74-20ff-5a85-bdd0-120e273b7e1a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-80cb97b9-3cce-5ddf-a76a-626c77b83982" :answer-field/choices ["answer-bb0e0e46-2dfb-5983-8032-25be5dcb57b8" "answer-4655fb84-1019-5281-8d52-c7b7aa2b05c1" "answer-10217f51-caf3-5dd4-96ac-8656fdc36c51" "answer-80cb97b9-3cce-5ddf-a76a-626c77b83982" "answer-3bbc2cd9-05ee-5ab2-b7f8-ecea5083e238"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bb0e0e46-2dfb-5983-8032-25be5dcb57b8" :answer/id #uuid "bb0e0e46-2dfb-5983-8032-25be5dcb57b8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{4\\sqrt{5}}{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4655fb84-1019-5281-8d52-c7b7aa2b05c1" :answer/id #uuid "4655fb84-1019-5281-8d52-c7b7aa2b05c1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\sqrt{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-10217f51-caf3-5dd4-96ac-8656fdc36c51" :answer/id #uuid "10217f51-caf3-5dd4-96ac-8656fdc36c51" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\sqrt{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-80cb97b9-3cce-5ddf-a76a-626c77b83982" :answer/id #uuid "80cb97b9-3cce-5ddf-a76a-626c77b83982" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3bbc2cd9-05ee-5ab2-b7f8-ecea5083e238" :answer/id #uuid "3bbc2cd9-05ee-5ab2-b7f8-ecea5083e238" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\sqrt{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186111867 :tutorial/content "Recall that the coordinates of a point on the unit circle with a central angle $\\theta$ have the property\n\n$$\nx = \\cos\\theta, \\qquad y = \\sin\\theta.\n$$\n\nIf this point is in the first quadrant, we can substitute these values into the Pythagorean Identity from before to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =1 \\\\ (\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2} & =1 \\\\ {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe last expression is known as the **Pythagorean trigonometric identity,** and is arguably the most important equation in trigonometry.\n\nThe Pythagorean trigonometric identity holds for *every* angle $\\theta,$ not just if $\\theta$ is in the first quadrant. In this lesson, we're focusing on the first quadrant only. We'll learn how to deal with angles in other quadrants in a future lesson." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186110825 :activity/expected-seconds 780.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id 17592186166887 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-ca88d8b7-77e6-50f5-9c7d-7baae6691eb5" "question-bf9b3381-1894-58e7-8430-4bb3204c16fe"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-ca88d8b7-77e6-50f5-9c7d-7baae6691eb5" :question/id #uuid "ca88d8b7-77e6-50f5-9c7d-7baae6691eb5" :question/math-academy-id "q-97507" :question/problem "Given that $X∼B(7,0.3),$ compute $P(X>5).$ Round your final answer to $4$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "For a binomial random variable $X∼B(n,p),$ where $n$ is the number of trials, and $p$ is the probability of success on each trial,  we have the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $X∼B(7,0.3),$ so $X$ has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{7}{x})(0.3)^{x}(0.7)^{7-x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>5) & =P(X\\in \\{6,7\\}) \\\\  & =f(6)+f(7) \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{7}{6})(0.3)^{6}(0.7)^{7-6}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{7}{7})(0.3)^{7}(0.7)^{7-7} \\\\  & =7(0.3)^{6}(0.7)+1(0.3)^{7}(0.7)^{0} \\\\  & ≈0.0038\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nto $4$ decimal places." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-7b2948cb-066b-5028-aa6d-bf771aec3f1b"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-7b2948cb-066b-5028-aa6d-bf771aec3f1b" :answer-field/id #uuid "7b2948cb-066b-5028-aa6d-bf771aec3f1b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-fe2ead44-1830-50be-bb8c-88e95281dd7b" :answer-field/choices ["answer-d415334a-6ee5-5b82-8191-f8fe0c93eadf" "answer-fe2ead44-1830-50be-bb8c-88e95281dd7b" "answer-19b8e472-101c-591c-9d12-ddb3ce4aa147" "answer-c86d3881-a5ac-5ea3-a168-a316059c706b" "answer-c0d4bf7c-79c0-59b1-b5ae-2e2017a9fe0d"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d415334a-6ee5-5b82-8191-f8fe0c93eadf" :answer/id #uuid "d415334a-6ee5-5b82-8191-f8fe0c93eadf" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.0012" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fe2ead44-1830-50be-bb8c-88e95281dd7b" :answer/id #uuid "fe2ead44-1830-50be-bb8c-88e95281dd7b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.0038" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-19b8e472-101c-591c-9d12-ddb3ce4aa147" :answer/id #uuid "19b8e472-101c-591c-9d12-ddb3ce4aa147" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.0162" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c86d3881-a5ac-5ea3-a168-a316059c706b" :answer/id #uuid "c86d3881-a5ac-5ea3-a168-a316059c706b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.0250" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c0d4bf7c-79c0-59b1-b5ae-2e2017a9fe0d" :answer/id #uuid "c0d4bf7c-79c0-59b1-b5ae-2e2017a9fe0d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.0288" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-bf9b3381-1894-58e7-8430-4bb3204c16fe" :question/id #uuid "bf9b3381-1894-58e7-8430-4bb3204c16fe" :question/math-academy-id "q-279683" :question/problem "If $X∼B(5,\\frac{2}{5}),$ then, **rounded to $3$ decimal places**, $P(X\\le 1)≈${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "For a binomial random variable $X∼B(n,p),$ where $n$ is the number of trials, and $p$ is the probability of success on each trial, we have the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $X∼B(5,\\frac{2}{5}),$ so $X$ has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{x}){(\\frac{2}{5})}^{x}{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{5-x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 1) & =P(X\\in \\{0,1\\}) \\\\  & =f(0)+f(1) \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{0}){(\\frac{2}{5})}^{0}{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{5-0}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{1}){(\\frac{2}{5})}^{1}{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{5-1} \\\\  & =1{(\\frac{2}{5})}^{0}{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{5}+5{(\\frac{2}{5})}^{1}{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{4} \\\\  & =0.337\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-66359473-563b-5acf-a793-afdba80eb7ff"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-66359473-563b-5acf-a793-afdba80eb7ff" :answer-field/id #uuid "66359473-563b-5acf-a793-afdba80eb7ff" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-de636aa5-cf99-5e6d-9779-b79decf69364" :answer-field/choices ["answer-de636aa5-cf99-5e6d-9779-b79decf69364"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-de636aa5-cf99-5e6d-9779-b79decf69364" :answer/id #uuid "de636aa5-cf99-5e6d-9779-b79decf69364" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.337" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186166888 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-1fdbae63-27df-5bf8-9dcb-6eda19028b94" "question-b40e7c83-4aaa-5842-b161-090408598820"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-1fdbae63-27df-5bf8-9dcb-6eda19028b94" :question/id #uuid "1fdbae63-27df-5bf8-9dcb-6eda19028b94" :question/math-academy-id "q-279711" :question/problem "If $X∼B(7,0.6),$ then, **rounded to $3$ decimal places**, $P(3<X<6)≈${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "For a binomial random variable $X∼B(n,p),$ where $n$ is the number of trials, and $p$ is the probability of success on each trial, we have the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $X∼B(7,0.6),$ so $X$ has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{7}{x}){(0.6)}^{x}{(0.4)}^{7-x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(3<X<6) & =P(X\\in \\{4,5\\}) \\\\  & =f(4)+f(5) \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{7}{4}){(0.6)}^{4}{(0.4)}^{7-4}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{7}{5}){(0.6)}^{5}{(0.4)}^{7-5} \\\\  & =35{(0.6)}^{4}{(0.4)}^{3}+21{(0.6)}^{5}{(0.4)}^{2} \\\\  & ≈0.552\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-7828d1b0-d4b0-550f-8d0b-d08471d793d0"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-7828d1b0-d4b0-550f-8d0b-d08471d793d0" :answer-field/id #uuid "7828d1b0-d4b0-550f-8d0b-d08471d793d0" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-f6a673f2-7816-549f-8fd7-7cd5ba2d59b7" :answer-field/choices ["answer-f6a673f2-7816-549f-8fd7-7cd5ba2d59b7"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f6a673f2-7816-549f-8fd7-7cd5ba2d59b7" :answer/id #uuid "f6a673f2-7816-549f-8fd7-7cd5ba2d59b7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.552" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-b40e7c83-4aaa-5842-b161-090408598820" :question/id #uuid "b40e7c83-4aaa-5842-b161-090408598820" :question/math-academy-id "q-279714" :question/problem "If $X∼B(5,\\frac{2}{3}),$  then, **rounded to $3$ decimal places**, $P(3\\le X<5)≈${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "For a binomial random variable $X∼B(n,p),$ where $n$ is the number of trials, and $p$ is the probability of success on each trial, we have the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $X∼B(5,\\frac{2}{3}),$ so $X$ has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{x}){(\\frac{2}{3})}^{x}{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{5-x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(3\\le X<5) & =P(X\\in \\{3,4\\}) \\\\  & =f(3)+f(4) \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{3}){(\\frac{2}{3})}^{3}{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{5-3}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{4}){(\\frac{2}{3})}^{4}{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{5-4} \\\\  & =10{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{3}{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}+5{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{4}{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{1} \\\\  & ≈0.658\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-7cb8eed7-dd98-5bd2-8807-1eee6e64325d"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-7cb8eed7-dd98-5bd2-8807-1eee6e64325d" :answer-field/id #uuid "7cb8eed7-dd98-5bd2-8807-1eee6e64325d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-f184934c-5ddc-5c09-89b2-c3b9969b08ab" :answer-field/choices ["answer-f184934c-5ddc-5c09-89b2-c3b9969b08ab"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f184934c-5ddc-5c09-89b2-c3b9969b08ab" :answer/id #uuid "f184934c-5ddc-5c09-89b2-c3b9969b08ab" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.658" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186166889 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-ca99c1c6-d952-5e5e-a558-22163d61daf7"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-ca99c1c6-d952-5e5e-a558-22163d61daf7" :question/id #uuid "ca99c1c6-d952-5e5e-a558-22163d61daf7" :question/math-academy-id "q-281207" :question/problem "If $X∼B(8,0.7),$ then, **rounded to $3$ decimal places**, $P(X\\le 6)≈${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "For a binomial random variable $X∼B(n,p),$ we have the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $X∼B(8,0.7),$ so $X$ has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{8}{x}){(0.7)}^{x}{(0.3)}^{8-x}.\n$$\n\nComputing $P(X\\le 6)$ will require us to compute $f(x)$ at $x=0,1,2,…,6.$ However, if we compute the complement $P(X>6)$ instead, then we will only have to compute $f(x)$ at $x=7,8,$ which will result in a much faster computation.\n\nComputing $P(X>6),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>6) & =P(X\\in \\{7,8\\}) \\\\  & =f(7)+f(8) \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{8}{7}){(0.7)}^{7}{(0.3)}^{8-7}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{8}{8}){(0.7)}^{8}{(0.3)}^{8-8} \\\\  & =8{(0.7)}^{7}{(0.3)}^{1}+1{(0.7)}^{8}{(0.3)}^{0} \\\\  & ≈0.255\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places.\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 6) & =1-P(X>6) \\\\  & ≈1-0.255 \\\\  & =0.745\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-9ea07dac-1cdf-58ab-b138-fc3a110b4291"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-9ea07dac-1cdf-58ab-b138-fc3a110b4291" :answer-field/id #uuid "9ea07dac-1cdf-58ab-b138-fc3a110b4291" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-a8ccf4bd-8a18-5847-ae19-0487fd1e69b0" :answer-field/choices ["answer-a8ccf4bd-8a18-5847-ae19-0487fd1e69b0"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a8ccf4bd-8a18-5847-ae19-0487fd1e69b0" :answer/id #uuid "a8ccf4bd-8a18-5847-ae19-0487fd1e69b0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.745" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "activity-38a625c6-f7b0-5f5a-addc-350bd81abfd9" :activity/id #uuid "38a625c6-f7b0-5f5a-addc-350bd81abfd9" :activity/title "The Binomial Distribution" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186050651 :activity/course 17592186046645 :activity/steps ["step-3453a0b7-17cb-5c5d-a3a2-4c5a26ff9daa" "step-901ed45f-da06-5711-b6bd-263161f1523b" "step-8cc84ca7-2774-520f-b516-b58e03200241" "step-354b6f11-1fbb-5f1b-bb7f-c47976a547b4" "step-3b09c256-75c6-53e9-926e-e65cea2f5b49"] :activity/first-step "step-3453a0b7-17cb-5c5d-a3a2-4c5a26ff9daa" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-3453a0b7-17cb-5c5d-a3a2-4c5a